John Carpenter's Halloween gave birth to  the modern slasher film establishing  virtually every one of the genres tropes  and dramatically announcing its director  as a major new talent but even though  you've seen it a dozen times there's a  lot you might not know about one of the  greatest and most influential horror  films ever made independent feature it's  now a classic horror film that paved the  way for everything that came after  but Halloween wasn't exactly a major  production the film was the brainchild  of producer Irwin Yablonski land ability  to innovate on a tight budget you  Blondes his idea was simple enough ah  movie about babysitters in jeopardy  being terrorized and we'll do it all in  one night in fact early in  pre-production the project had the  working title the babysitter murders  speaking with Entertainment Weekly  Carpenter remembered I was finishing  directing a TV movie called someone's  watching me when Earl enya blonds called  and said why don't we set it on  Halloween night in fact why don't we  call it Halloween Carpenter came up with  the idea of the small town with a dark  secret that's revisited on Halloween  night he and Debra hill wrote the script  as he'll explained to Fangoria we wrote  the whole thing in three weeks then we  just had to find the right actors  casting Laurie especially important was  casting the part of Laurie Strode the  movies heroine Tommy Lee Wallace  carpenters friend and one of the film's  editors explained a consequence of sound  this was a time that women were  asserting their rights like never before  and Deborah was a very assertive woman  no way she was going to have a weeping  violet type as her heroine carpenter who  didn't watch television had never seen  Jamie Lee Curtis work on the series  operation petticoat  but Hill was convinced she'd be perfect  for the role one small detail was all it  took to convince carpenter Curtis also  happened to be the daughter of Janet Lee  perhaps the original scream queen whose  shower had been rudely interrupted in  the 1960 classic psycho pulling out all  the tricks the film was shot with a  $320,000 budget if it doesn't look  particularly low-budget it's because  Carpenter was a master at making  something from nothing as there was no  room in the budget for a costume  department the entire cast wardrobe was  purchased on the cheap at local  department stores Michael Myers mask was  crafted from a repurposed William  Shatner mask purchased from a local  costume store for a dollar 98 and  painted white also enabling them to keep  the budget  check the filming location for the Myers  house was an abandoned property owned by  a church and although the film was shot  in Pasadena California it was set in  Illinois so carpenter evokes the feel of  autumn in the Midwest by using the same  several trash bags full of painted  leaves for only exterior scenes  scattering them around whatever street  they were currently shooting on only to  collect them all to be reused in the  next shot the minimalist aesthetic  carried over to post-production  Carpenter wrote the film's classic theme  in only an hour and scored the entire  film himself in just three days working  for peanuts another way to keep the  budget low was to pay very little as the  biggest name in the cast Donald  Pleasence got a flat fee of $20,000  while a little-known star Kurtis  received only $8,000 carpenter's buddy  Nick Cassell was paid the princely sum  of $25 per day to portray the shape as  Michael Myers is called in the credits  and carpenter himself took only $10,000  plus a small percentage of the film's  profits which ended up working out for  him he said I can't remember exactly how  much money I made off the film I do  remember getting one check for over a  million dollars that wasn't bad limited  release Halloween debuted on October  25th 1978  in a single City Kansas City Missouri as  the bus started to grow national critics  started paying attention the great Roger  Ebert concluded his sparkling review  with a warning that should have had any  horror fans sprinting out the door to  buy tickets I'd like to be clear about  this  if you don't want to have a really  terrifying experience don't see  Halloween the film went on to gross in  astonishing 47 million dollars raining  for over a decade as the most profitable  independent film ever made  not as gory as you think despite how you  might remember it  Halloween includes virtually no on  screen gore carpenter's expert use of  slowly building tension spooky imagery  and perfectly deployed jump scares left  audiences with the impression that what  they had just seen must have been crazy  violent in order to be that disturbing  but that wasn't carpenters take I wanted  something deadly simple and something  that would just eat a little bit Hill  alluded to this in production notes for  1981 s Halloween to sane people don't  seem to realize that we showed next to  no blood in the first picture you think  you're seeing a lot more than we're  showing you the almost anthology  carpenter and he'll never envision  even a single sequel let alone a  franchise carpenter explained a deadline  I didn't think there was any more story  and I didn't want to do it again instead  of finding ways to bring Michael Myers  back carpenter and Hill had the idea to  tell a different story with each new  Halloween film making it an ongoing  horror anthology series Halloween Sue's  tepid box office convinced producers to  give it a shot and the result 1982's  Halloween 3 season of the witch  instantly polarized horror fans  everywhere many fans wanted Michael  Myers back and they got their wish six  years later  although the sequels and rebooted series  that follow provided mostly diminishing  returns with one exception  Halloween h2o 20 years later ignored  previous sequels and served as a direct  sequel to Halloween - ironically  Halloween is set to receive the same  retcon treatment again the Halloween  reboot scheduled for 2018 will serve as  a direct sequel to the original with  carpenters serving as executive producer  and providing the score and Jamie Lee  Curtis returning to play Laurie Strode  one more time  [Music]
 

